  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Wed Jun 24 2026\n\nSubtropical high pressure aloft fixed directly over the El Paso \nregion today, which will allow this ongoing heat wave to reach a \nhead this afternoon. Forecast high of 107 degrees for El Paso, \nwith the rest of the NM lowlands 102 to 106 degrees. Heat Advisory\nremains in effect for most of southern New Mexico and far west \nTexas. Slight increase in moisture should also allow for isolated \n(10-20% coverage) rain showers. These will be almost exclusively\ndriven by convective temperature forcing due to the heat, but may\nbe further assisted by weak shortwaves aloft. Surface dewpoint \ndepressions of 70+ degrees (a lot of 105/35 this afternoon) and \nvery weak instability (CAPE 50-200 J/kg) indicates that any \nshowers that form will be weak and low-precipitation. Virga will \nbe more common, leading to a dry microburst threat and the \npotential for gusty winds this afternoon.\n\nUpper high sags south slightly on Thursday, which will begin a\n\"cooling\" trend. Lowlands highs will remain above 100 into the\nweekend, however. Isolated (20-30%) showers and thunderstorms\nThursday afternoon will be low-precipitation with strong wind\ngusts being the primary threat.\n\nJet stream flow over the Pacific coast increases over the \nweekend, which will push the upper high eastward and flush \nmoisture eastward as well. Storm chances will be limited mostly \neast of the Rio Grande and over far west Texas. Temperatures will\ncontinue to drop a few degrees each day, with the forecast ending\nour current streak of triple-digit heat by early next week.\n\nStagnant weather pattern next week, with a trough across the\nWestern U.S. and a strong upper high across the Mississippi River\nValley. The El Paso region will sit in the middle of these\nfeatures, leading to favorable south flow and monsoonal moisture\nin place. Rain chances look pretty good area-wide the first week\nof July with scattered thunderstorm chances each afternoon.",
